Most Windows services run as **LocalSystem** (SYSTEM), the most powerful local identity (windows-for-security). A service's configuration names a file to run. If an ordinary user can influence *which file* runs, they get SYSTEM the next time the service starts.

There are three ways that happens, and ATT&CK gives each its own entry:
- the path is ambiguous because it has spaces and no quotes ([T1574.009](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/009/));
- the file or its folder is writable by users ([T1574.010](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/010/)), which you met as the AcmeAgent folder in windows-for-security;
- the service's own configuration, its security descriptor or registry key, can be changed by users ([T1574.011](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/011/)).

**What it leaves behind.** Each route changes something, and Sysmon (windows-for-security) records most of it:
- a new or overwritten executable in a program folder: Sysmon **event 11** (FileCreate) with the process that wrote it;
- a changed `ImagePath`: Sysmon **event 13** (registry value set) under the Services key;
- the payoff: at the next service start, a process whose parent is `services.exe` running from an unexpected path or with a new hash (Security **4688** or Sysmon **event 1**, which records hashes).

**Prevent:** quote every service path, keep installers from loosening ACLs under Program Files, grant service rights only to administrators, and run both audits on every new image and after software installs. Application control that only allows signed, known binaries to run as services blocks the payoff even when a path is wrong.

**What real attackers looked for.** The Conti advisory lists the discovery behaviour of Conti ransomware itself:
- reading the local ARP cache (the table of recently seen neighbours on the network) with the `GetIpNetTable()` API, and checking that targets are local, non-internet addresses (T1016);
- listing network connections (T1049) and processes, looking for any with "sql" in the name (T1057), because databases are worth encrypting;
- enumerating SMB shares on other machines with `NetShareEnum()` ([T1135](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1135/)).

In the HSE intrusion, PwC's timeline records "AD and domain reconnaissance" on 7 May 2021, the same day the attacker first used highly privileged accounts and compromised servers. Discovery came right before the damage spread, which makes it one of the best places to catch an intrusion.

